<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transylvanian Saxons</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Transylvanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> are a German-speaking people settled in southern and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transylvania">Transylvania</a> from the mid-twelfth century, first under King Géza II. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diploma-andreanum">Andreanum</a> of 1224 granted them collective self-government, and their towns, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sibiu">Hermannstadt</a> (Sibiu) and Kronstadt (Brașov), and their fortified churches flourished. Most emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> and the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/communism">communism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transylvanian-saxons.jpg" alt="Transylvanian Saxons" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Argean · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Transylvania_Saxons.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…as frontier warriors, Muslim and Jewish merchants handled trade and sometimes royal finances, and from the twelfth century Germans, Walloons and Italians were invited as ‘guests’ (hospites) with their own liberties; Géza II settled the <b>Saxons of Transylvania</b>, whose rights Andrew II confirmed in the Andreanum of 1224.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1000-1301">Kingdom of Hungary (1000-1301) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Late medieval Hungary was a large, thinly populated kingdom of probably around three million people by 1500, including Magyars, Slovaks, Croats, Romanians, Germans, Ruthenians and Serbs, as well as the <b>Saxons of Transylvania</b> and the Szeklers and Cumans, who held distinct collective rights.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1301-1526">Kingdom of Hungary (1301-1526) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Transylvanian Saxons</b>, angered when Vlad III strengthened Wallachia's borders and disrupted their trade routes, spread grotesque poems painting him as a drinker of blood; those tales inspired the main character of Bram Stoker's Dracula in 1897.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-wallachia">Principality of Wallachia · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
